# Cinakey — Source of Truth

## 1. Product
Cinakey is the studio pipeline for AI filmmaking: one browser workspace that takes a project from idea to exported cut. Competitors (LTX Studio, Google Flow, Higgsfield) sell access to models; Cinakey wins on pipeline depth: the film is structured, versioned data (script, shots, takes, assets, edit decisions) that the AI copilot can reason over and that can be exported cleanly.
Users: solo creators first (YouTubers, indie filmmakers, ad and social makers); studios and agencies later (phase 3). Core job: "Turn my idea into a finished, consistent video without juggling ten tools or losing track of what worked."
North-star metric: finished minutes exported per active workspace per month.
Built by a solo developer using AI-assisted coding.

## 2. Principles
1. Pipeline over model count: one model per job at launch (Seedance 2.5 video, GPT Image 2 images, DeepSeek copilot) behind model-neutral adapters so more can be added without product changes.
2. Everything is a linked record: every asset knows its project, scene, shot and the job, prompt and references that made it.
3. Nothing is lost: scripts, sheets, shots, blockouts and timelines are versioned with parent pointers; users compare, branch and roll back.
4. The AI works on the project: the copilot reads project data and proposes changes as diffs with cost estimates; the user accepts or rejects.
5. Open at the edges: import any media; export MP4 and JSON (later OTIO/XML for Premiere, Resolve, Final Cut).

## 3. Tech stack and hard rules
- Front end: React + TypeScript (strict), Vite, hosted as a static site (Vercel, Netlify or Cloudflare Pages).
- Back end: Convex (database, queries, mutations, actions, scheduled functions, HTTP actions, file storage, text and vector search). No separate API server.
- Repository: npm workspaces monorepo (`apps/*`, `packages/*`) with Turborepo. `apps/web` is the creator app (`@cinakey/web`); `packages/backend` is the Convex project (`@cinakey/backend`); `packages/shared` holds cross-cutting types (`@cinakey/shared`); `packages/config` holds shared tsconfig / ESLint / Prettier (`@cinakey/config`). Later: `apps/admin` and `packages/ui` (not created yet).
- Auth: Convex Auth (email and password, magic links, Google OAuth). Staff accounts are flagged separately from customers.
- Secrets: provider keys (SEEDANCE_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, DEEPSEEK_API_KEY, etc.) are Convex environment variables, read via process.env only inside actions. Nothing secret in VITE_ variables or the repo.
- Providers: Seedance 2.5 via ByteDance's base API (video, upscale, extend); GPT Image 2 (images, edits); DeepSeek (copilot LLM).
- Storage: Convex file storage behind ONE storage module at `packages/backend/convex/storage.ts` (and matching helpers). Nothing else touches storage directly, so video can later move to Cloudflare R2 (free egress) by changing that module only.
- Convex documents are limited to 1 MiB: large JSON (blockout scenes, Theatre.js state, timeline snapshots) is stored as files; the database row holds the file ID.
- Rendering happens in the browser: WebCodecs for encode/decode, WebGL/WebGPU for compositing, low-res proxies for playback. No render server in the MVP.
- 3D blockout: three.js used imperatively (scene, mannequins, props, lights, cameras; OrbitControls + TransformControls) + `@theatre/core` (keyframed animation per shot; project state generated from Cinakey keyframe tracks, no Theatre Studio UI). MP4 export uses WebCodecs `VideoEncoder` + `mp4-muxer`; multi-file exports are zipped with JSZip.
- Real-time comes from Convex live queries (job status, notifications, later presence and shot locks). Yjs for co-editing in phase 3.

## 4. Pipeline
Brief -> Script -> Look development -> Blockout -> Shot generation -> Edit -> Review -> Export. Each stage's output is the next stage's structured input; users can enter at any stage (e.g. upload footage and start at Edit). Review notes loop back to single shots (regenerate or swap a take).

## 5. Modules and features
MVP items are marked [MVP]; others note their phase.
1. Script room [MVP]: screenplay and A/V formats via a custom structured editor over `cinakey.script/1.0` JSON (versioned on `scriptVersions.contentFileId`); scenes, beats and dialogue lines are addressable records with stable UUIDs inside that document; live `scenes` rows mirror script scenes via `elementId` for shot linking; runtime estimate (dialogue ~150 wpm + action padding) vs `targetLengthSec`; entity extraction (characters, locations, props) into look-dev `entities` with name-based linking that survives renames; script versions with parent pointers, side-by-side structural diff, and restore-as-new-version; shots with a linked `dialogueLineId` flagged `outdated` when that line's text changes or the line is removed.
2. Look development [MVP]: character, **creature**, location, prop and style sheets as `cinakey.sheet/1.0` JSON on `entities.sheetFileId` (versioned via new blobs + `parentFileId`); character/creature identity slots (front, three-quarter, profile, full body) and expression variants; location/prop hero locks; style palette/lighting/lens/grain/mood plus **`artStyleBlock`** (project ART STYLE paragraph injected into every prompt template); `referenceSheetAssetId` for pipeline-locked composite reference images; GPT Image 2 text-to-image, variations, identity-set (4 jobs), and mask inpaint via the Phase 3 job runner (`entityId` on jobs/assets); import photos with `likenessConsent` when depicting a real face; `lockedReferenceAssetIds` denormalized from sheet slots.
3. Blockout [MVP]: shot list table per scene (order, shot type, lens mm, camera move, duration, characters, location, linked dialogue line, status `planned`|`blocked_out`|`generating`|`selected`, notes) with inline edit, drag reorder, split/merge; `outdated` boolean badge when a linked `dialogueLineId` changes in a new script version; storyboard grid with one `keyframeAssetId` per shot (GPT Image 2 via jobs, upload, or sketch); total shot duration vs script scene estimate; copilot `propose_shot_list`. 3D blockout editor per shot (route `/projects/:projectId/blockout/shots/:shotId`): ground and set pieces; mannequins (standing / sitting / walking) tagged with the shot's characters; props (box, chair, table, door, car); key / fill / back lights; a shot camera whose lens mm comes from the shot; orbit view, look-through-shot-camera toggle with aspect-ratio frame and thirds guides, move / rotate / scale gizmos; keyframes for camera, mannequins and props with timeline scrubber and play/pause; starts from the storyboard keyframe shown as an adjustable reference overlay. Save writes a new blockout file version and moves `planned` shots to `blocked_out`. "Render guides" saves a keyframe image and a depth pass as shot assets (tags `guide` + `blockout-keyframe` / `blockout-depth`) for Phase 8 generation. Export one shot, a scene or the whole project as JSON and/or browser-encoded MP4 at project fps (optional burn-in of shot number, scene and timecode; one sequence or a clip per shot; progress and cancel; "both" downloads a zip); shots without a 3D blockout are skipped and reported. Import a `cinakey.blockout` JSON file into a shot (the previous blockout stays as a parent version). Animatic timeline is phase 2.
4. Shot generation [MVP]: prompts assembled from shot, character, location and style data; multiple takes per shot, one selected; start/end frames, references and camera presets as far as Seedance 2.5 supports (controls come from the adapter's capability manifest); upscale and extend via Seedance; fixing = regenerate as a new take or trim in the editor; every take records model and version. Batch scene generation and lip sync are phase 2.
5. Browser editor [MVP, in-house]: multi-track timeline (video, dialogue, music, SFX, titles); cut, trim, split, reorder; uploaded and generated clips side by side; swap take / regenerate from the timeline; music upload, captions; in-browser MP4 export. Aspect-ratio variants, colour/LUTs and OTIO/XML export are phase 2.
6. Asset library [MVP]: upload footage, photos, audio, music and logos (MIME + size validation via the storage module); typed assets linked to project/scene/shot/job with display `name` and searchable tags; grid and list views; filters by type, tag, character (via shot), shot; text search (Convex search index on name+tags); detail drawer with preview, metadata, tags, and lineage (job, model, prompt, seed, parents); star, rename, tag, delete with confirm; per-project and cross-project library. Selected takes kept; rejected takes purged after a set period unless starred (purge deferred).
7. AI copilot [MVP]: DeepSeek via its API behind an adapter; **Copilot tab** (first project tab) is a full-page view with conversation + pipeline panel (Story → Asset sheets → Script prompts → Blockout sheets); right-side chat panel remains on other project pages and shares the same `copilotMessages` thread; streaming via live-updated messages; aware of current view, selection and project (brief, tip script, entities, rules, live scenes/shot counts, prompt-sheet pipeline status, art style block); primed as film director, screenwriter and character designer (auto from view, user override) with modes brainstorm / critique / pacing / continuity; acts only through tools that create `proposals` shown as cards with diffs and cost estimates — Accept / Edit / Reject. Tools: `propose_script_edit`, `propose_entities`, `propose_rules`, `propose_character_details`, `propose_image_prompt`, `propose_shot_list`, plus Phase 7C pipeline tools `propose_story_treatment`, `propose_style_block`, `propose_asset_list`, `propose_asset_sheet`, `revise_asset_sheet`, `propose_script_prompt`, `propose_blockout_sheet`, `apply_blockout_sheet`. Accept materializes script versions, entities/rules, character sheets, draft prompts, shot lists, story treatments, style ART STYLE blocks, structured prompt sheets, sequences/shots, and 3D blockouts; asset-sheet accept can queue GPT Image 2. Project rules list (`projects.rules`); conversation history per project; each turn reserved/settled on the credits ledger via slim `generationJobs` kind `chat`. Deferred: queue video generations (Phase 8), multi-thread chat, mobile side-panel Sheet.
8. Platform essentials: cost estimates, batch confirmation, spend caps [MVP]; in-app and email job notifications, automatic retries and credit refunds [MVP]; guided starter template [MVP]; internal admin via Convex dashboard plus protected staff pages [MVP]; share links (phase 2); project members with Owner/Editor/Commenter/Viewer roles and shot locks (phase 2). Projects dashboard (create from brief, rename, shallow duplicate, soft-archive) and project workspace with pipeline stage tabs + derived progress [MVP].
9. Admin platform (phase 2): separate React app in `apps/admin` on the same Convex deployment; areas for users, projects, jobs/providers, billing/credits, moderation, models/pricing, analytics, admin users, audit log; RBAC roles (Super admin, Operations admin, Support, Moderator, Finance, Engineer, Analyst) built from fine-grained permissions; every admin function checks permissions server-side; append-only audit log.

## 6. Data model (Convex tables)
MVP ownership: each user has a personal `workspace`; every `project` belongs to a workspace; access = workspace owner (or `users.isStaff`). `projectMembers` / share links / admin RBAC come later.

Versioning: `scriptVersions` and `timelineVersions` insert new rows with optional `parentId` (never overwrite content in place). Script body is `cinakey.script/1.0` JSON in file storage (`contentFileId`); beats and dialogue lines live in that document with stable string ids (not separate Convex tables in MVP). Look-dev sheets are `cinakey.sheet/1.0` JSON blobs; `entities.sheetFileId` points at the current file and each save writes a new blob with `parentFileId`. Blockout JSON is a new storage blob per save (`cinakey.blockout/1.0` single-shot document with `version` and `parentFileId`); `shots.blockoutFileId` points at the current file. **Prompt sheets** (`promptSheets`) version via tip rows (`isTip`, `parentId`, `version`) with structured JSON in storage. Live working records (`scenes`, `shots`, `sequences` metadata) may be patched; `scenes.elementId` links a live scene to its script scene id. Large JSON always lives in file storage; documents hold only storage IDs (`sizeBytes` required on assets). Credits are per workspace (`creditLedger` append-only). Soft-archive projects via `archivedAt` (no hard delete in MVP). Copilot conversation is one linear thread per project (`copilotMessages`); tool outputs become `proposals` until the user accepts or rejects.

